        ":question/id": "a8381fb3-8431-5be5-b51b-a5d43ddf8c5f",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-41817",
        ":question/problem": "Given that $F=2x{y}^{2}\\,i+4{x}^{2}y\\,j,$ what is $\\text{div}\\,F?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =2{y}^{2}+4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
