        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-123173",
        ":question/problem": "Consider the vector field $F=({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,i-({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot F=\\text{div}\\,F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{e}^{x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y). \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point  $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
