        ":question/id": "ca50a185-e281-53e0-900c-05cc2ac6e7a5",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-105562",
        ":question/problem": "Consider the vector field $F={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(\\pi ,1).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(\\pi ,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(\\pi ,1) & =2(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )+\\frac{\\pi }{1} \\\\  & =2(1)(1)+\\pi  \\\\  & =2+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      }
    ],
